Logistics:


  1. When will a formal code inspection be warranted?

Prior to every milestone

  2. Who will take the lead on moderating inspections?

Alternate between everybody, ideally the moderator would not have worked extensively on the chunk of code being reviewed

  3. How will you share the results of inspections?

During an in-person team meeting or over MS teams, describing all the recommended changes

Criteria:


  4. What are the key "code smells" from each chapter of Clean Code? This is the big question. Chapter 17 of Clean Code might help you recall them.

Names:
	- Non-descriptive names
	- Unpronounceable/unsearchable names
	- Unrelated names

Functions:
	- Non descriptive function names
	- Large functions
	- Have many purposes and not understandable

Comments:
	- Comments that just repeat exactly what the code say
	- Misleading
	- Try to make up for bad/gross code
	- Large chunks of comments "noise"

Formatting:
	- Too much/too little spacing (density)
	- Inconsistent formatting

Demeter:
	- Have beyond the scope of the class and the arguments passed in
	- "Trains" of functional calls

Error Handling:
	- Not separating error handling logic
	- Error handler in method that throws the error
	- Throwing already checked exceptions
	- Returning null

Unit Testing:
	- Tests are not clean
	- Setup is not separated from asserts
	- Tests are not domain specific/class specific
	- Tests do not check edge cases

Classes:
	- Large
	- Methods only contain 1-2 fields
	- Using getters/setters in objects
	- Hybrid classes

Emergence:
	- Not running all the tests
	- Duplicated code
	- Does not minimize the number of classes


  5. Will everyone apply all criteria from every chapter from Clean Code? Or will each person specialize in a few criteria?

We will rotate between who looks at what classes and we will keep all of the criteria in mind when inspecting the code.


Scope:


  6. Will your team inspect every file in your codebase? Every file you touch in your feature branch? Or something else entirely?

We will inspect every file touched/edited in the feature branch

  7. Of those files, will each person look at every file in consideration? Or will your team assign different files to different people?

We will plan to look at separate files, but rotate between reviews to make sure the same person does not get the same file multiple weeks in a row.

Tools:

  8. To what extent can your inspection criteria be automated? Automation will increase your inspection's speed and reliability.

SpotBugs can be used to solve a few of the issues.

  9. Which aspects of your inspection criteria will need human intervention?

Even after SpotBugs we will have to proofread edited files and check for issues that SpotBugs did not already find.
